    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1412">

                <pb facs="1518" n="1504" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg1504"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">823.248/354</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Chief, Division of West
                        Coast Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lyon</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic">to the Director, Office of
                        American Republic Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Duggan</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-02-25">February 25, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Duggan</hi>: Colonel Revoredo<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d1412-f1"/> is probably coming to complain to you about the action
                    of the Munitions Assignment Committee “Air” in turning down an application by
                    Peru on February 2 for twelve dive bombers. The refusal to allow Peru to receive
                    these planes is in keeping with our policy which is not to supply combat <pb
                        facs="1519" n="1505" xml:id="pg1505"/> planes to the other Americas which
                    are not actually engaged in the war. I believe the only exceptions are Mexico
                    and Brazil.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d1412-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>What I had in mind when I spoke to you was the attached letter from Mr.
                        Bonsal<anchor n="5" xml:id="d1412-f3"/> to Jeff Patterson<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d1412-f4"/> concerning a military mission for Peru, which Patterson
                    discussed when he was up here last week. I now think it unlikely, however, that
                    Revoredo would be discussing this as it seems to be a matter originating with
                    Colonel Johnson.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d1412-f5"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1412-f1" n="3" xml:id="d1412-f1-content">
                <p>Col. Armando Revoredo, Peruvian Air Attaché in Washington.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1412-f2" n="4" xml:id="d1412-f2-content">
                <p>For correspondence on the development of a revised policy on sending weapons to
                    the American Republics, see <ref target="#pg87">pp. 87</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1412-f3" n="5" xml:id="d1412-f3-content">
                <p>Philip W. Bonsal, Deputy Director, Office of American Republic Affairs; letter
                    not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1412-f4" n="6" xml:id="d1412-f4-content">
                <p>Jefferson Patterson, Counselor of Embassy in Peru.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1412-f5" n="7" xml:id="d1412-f5-content">
                <p>Presumably Col. Bayard A. C. Johnson.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
